# A spherical lamp. THIS SCENE DOES NOT LOAD YET.
#
#   image_stats: an emissive material sits on a `sphere`, which cannot hand out points of itself:
#   it would be seen and would light nothing
#
# `Sphere` does not implement `AreaSampleable`, so `SceneBuilderVisitor` refuses the description
# rather than render a lamp that lights nothing. The file is here because it is the witness the
# work that lifts that restriction will be measured on, and because writing it afterwards would
# mean measuring a change against a scene invented to suit it. See `ideas/area_light.md` §7.
#
#   --integrator naive|path --renderer mt --threads 8 --seed 0
#   --samples_ppx 1024 --max_depth 4 --output_width 256 --output_height 192
#
# WHAT IT WILL ISOLATE
#
# The same question as `direct_lighting.stage` — do `naive` and `path` agree — on the first source
# that is not flat. It is deliberately that scene with the panel swapped for a sphere of the same
# span and the camera left where it was, so the two figures can be read against each other.
#
# WHY A SPHERE IS NOT A RECTANGLE WITH A DIFFERENT SHAPE
#
# A point drawn uniformly over the area of a sphere lands on the far side half of the time, and
# every one of those is dropped: emission is one-sided, so `cos θₗ ≤ 0` answers "no sample" before
# any density is computed. Half the draws are therefore spent to produce nothing, whatever the
# sphere's size or distance — a waste the rectangle has no equivalent of, since every point of a
# flat source faces the same way.
#
# That is why pbrt samples the cone the sphere subtends from the shaded point rather than its area,
# and it makes this scene the one where the difference between the two draws shows without any
# grazing geometry being arranged. A uniform-area draw that is merely *correct* here is already a
# result; that it is wasteful is the measurement to take.

camera pin_hole
  pos 0.0 2.9 0.0
  look 0.0 0.0 0.0
  up 0.0 0.0 -1.0

scene

  object simple
    rectangle 12 12
    lambertian color 0.6 0.6 0.6

  # The lamp. A sphere emits through every point of its surface, so unlike a `rectangle` it needs
  # no rotation to be aimed: there is no face to choose.
  object transformed
    object simple
      sphere 1.0
      diffuse_light color 6.0 6.0 6.0
    transform {
      translate 0.0 3.0 0.0
    }
